Beyond Turing: A Multidimensional Cognitive Evaluation Framework for Artificial Intelligence

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Kimi 

Abstract

The Turing test’s binary, imitation‑based paradigm is inadequate for assessing the multifaceted cognitive capabilities of modern AI systems. This revision of the ECP‑B framework addresses identified weaknesses in earlier formulations by (i) reorganizing nine cognitive dimensions into three conceptually distinct tiers—object‑level competence, meta‑level cognition, and system‑level development—each aggregated with a different power‑mean parameter grounded in risk theory rather than mere convention; (ii) explicitly modeling inter‑dimensional dependencies through covariance‑aware aggregation and a residualization procedure that is made compatible with geometric aggregation; (iii) adding a formal sensitivity analysis for the aggregation parameter and weight vector, including a closed‑form bound for the former and a vertex reduction for the latter; (iv) confronting the problem of Goodhart’s law and benchmark contamination through mandatory task rotation, private held‑out validation, and a contamination risk‑tiering protocol; and (v) grounding weight selection in either expert elicitation or the Benefit‑of‑the‑Doubt (BoD) formulation of Data Envelopment Analysis, with explicit treatment of its self‑rating weakness via cross‑efficiency scoring. The framework retains its commitment to uncertainty quantification but now supplements the delta method with conformal prediction for safety‑critical dimensions and provides a Safety‑Envelope Composite for deployment decisions. The result is a more defensible, extensible, and policy‑relevant architecture for multidimensional AI evaluation.

Keywords: AI evaluation, cognitive architecture, composite indicators, uncertainty quantification, power mean, Goodhart’s law, benchmark contamination, data envelopment analysis, conformal prediction, non‑compensatory aggregation

---

1. Introduction

The assessment of artificial intelligence has progressed from single‑task benchmarks to multidimensional batteries, yet fundamental challenges remain unaddressed. Prior frameworks—including the original ECP‑B proposal—implicitly treated cognitive dimensions as orthogonal and commensurable, relied on fixed public task suites vulnerable to contamination, and presented aggregation choices as neutral technical parameters rather than value‑laden decisions. Recent literature on composite indicators demonstrates that the choice of weighting and aggregation is the primary source of index illegitimacy, and that geometric aggregation is only a partial solution to compensability. In AI, the accelerating degradation of public benchmarks—where frontier models now exceed 90% on many standard tests and no known mitigation strategy reliably prevents contamination—demands a fundamentally different approach.

This revised framework addresses these problems directly. We retain the nine cognitive dimensions but reorganize them into three tiers based on their epistemic character and risk profile: (1) Competence—object‑level abilities observable in task performance; (2) Metacognition & Learning—self‑referential capabilities that regulate and improve competence; and (3) Robustness & Scalability—system‑level properties that constrain safe deployment. Each tier is aggregated with a different power‑mean exponent whose choice is justified by a risk‑theoretic argument (Section 3.3). Dimension scores are no longer assumed independent; we introduce an explicit covariance matrix and propagate its effect through the aggregation. The weight vector is justified via expert elicitation or replaced by a non‑parametric Data Envelopment Analysis (DEA) approach that avoids fixed a priori weights. The framework incorporates mandatory anti‑gaming provisions: periodic rotation of task suites, private held‑out validation sets, and contamination auditing. Finally, we add a Safety‑Envelope Composite that maps the weakest tier score to deployment autonomy levels, providing a single binding constraint for risk assessment.

The remainder of this document is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews related work and corrects several citation inaccuracies from earlier drafts. Section 3 establishes the mathematical foundations, including the risk‑theoretic justification for aggregation parameters, covariance‑aware uncertainty propagation, sensitivity analysis, and weight justification. Section 4 presents the revised three‑tier dimension structure with explicit internal aggregation rules and a dependence‑adjustment procedure that is compatible with geometric aggregation. Section 5 describes the architecture of the evaluation battery, including the anti‑gaming protocol and the Safety‑Envelope Composite. Section 6 discusses theoretical limitations and future extensions, and Section 7 concludes.

