2. Background and Related Work

2.1 The Turing Test and Its Successors

The Turing test (Turing, 1950) evaluated machine intelligence through indistinguishable imitation in conversation. Its shortcomings—anthropocentrism, focus on deception, and lack of quantitative rigor—are well documented (Searle, 1980). Modern benchmarks such as GLUE, SuperGLUE, MMLU, BIG‑bench, and ARC have provided quantitative measures for narrow capabilities, but they are increasingly compromised by training‑data contamination and benchmark overfitting. Recent work (Sun et al., 2026) tested 20 mitigation strategies across 10 models and 5 benchmarks and found none significantly improved contamination resistance; even canary‑string protections failed (BIG‑bench’s canary GUID was ingested into GPT‑4’s training data). This reality forces any credible evaluation framework to treat public benchmarks as inherently suspect and to require private, held‑out, dynamically rotated tasks.

2.2 Multidimensional Frameworks

Recent policy and research initiatives have recognized the need for broader assessment. The OECD AI Capability Indicators (OECD, 2025) define nine capability domains (Language, Social interaction, Problem solving, Creativity, Metacognition, Knowledge/learning/memory, Vision, Manipulation, Robotic intelligence) on five‑level scales toward human equivalence; a draft tenth indicator (“Consciousness”) exists but is not yet included in the beta release. The CAIE Framework (CAIE Consortium, 2026) organizes over 90 fine‑grained cognitive features into six zones and uses a two‑stage scoring system (ZUCS/SZUCS) with maturity gaps; its granularity is high but mathematical cohesion is limited. NIST’s ARIA program (ARIA, 2025) undertakes multi‑tier evaluation through model testing, red‑teaming, and field testing—a qualitative pipeline, not a weighted composite. The original ECP‑B framework attempted to unify these efforts with a mathematically rigorous power‑mean aggregation and uncertainty propagation, but failed to address the normative content of aggregation, dimension interdependence, and the gaming problem.

This revision builds on the strengths of all these approaches while correcting the identified weaknesses.

