5. Architecture of the Revised Evaluation Battery

5.1 Task Portfolio and Anti‑Gaming Provisions

Because public benchmarks are contaminated, the evaluation battery must include:

· Private held‑out task suites for each dimension, developed by an independent evaluation consortium and never released publicly. These are used for official scoring; public benchmarks may be used for development but not for certification.
· Periodic rotation: any benchmark suite whose mean solve rate among top frontier models exceeds 90% is deprecated from the basket in the next cycle and replaced with newly constructed, out‑of‑distribution tasks. Replacement tasks are validated for construct relevance and difficulty calibration.
· Contamination risk tiering: Each task item is assigned a risk level:
  · Green: No detectable leakage via n‑gram overlap, embedding similarity, and perplexity divergence.
  · Yellow: Marginal signals (e.g., perplexity slightly below baseline); task retained but flagged, and its contribution to the dimension score is down‑weighted.
  · Red: Confirmed leakage; task excluded.
  This replaces the earlier binary “no detectable leakage” claim, which was too strong given that detection methods have false‑negative rates. To limit the impact of undetected contamination, no single task contributes more than 20% to its dimension score.
· Adversarial evaluation: a separate red team attempts to exploit the evaluation protocol by overfitting to the published scoring rubric or by gaming the metrics. Results from adversarial evaluation are reported alongside official scores.

5.2 Scoring and Aggregation

For a given system, raw scores are collected per dimension using both public (development) and private (certification) tasks. After normalization and uncertainty estimation, we obtain a vector  \mathbf{D}  and covariance  \Sigma . We then compute tier scores as described in Section 4. For each tier, we also compute its CBI and a sensitivity interval based on variations of  p  and weights.

The final report includes:

· Three tier scores with 95% confidence intervals.
· Tier‑specific CBIs.
· A dependence‑adjusted composite (if residualization applied).
· Sensitivity ranges indicating the robustness of interpretation bands.
· A contamination audit summary.
· (For Tier 3) Conformal prediction intervals for safety‑critical metrics.

5.3 Safety‑Envelope Composite and Deployment Autonomy

For deployment certification, we define a Safety‑Envelope Composite:

S_{\text{safe}} = \min\left( S_{\text{Tier1}}^{(p=0)},\; S_{\text{Tier2}}^{(p=1)},\; S_{\text{Tier3}}^{(p=-1)} \right).

This weakest‑link aggregation reflects the principle that a system is only as safe as its most deficient tier. Autonomy levels are mapped to  S_{\text{safe}}  bands:

· Tool:  S_{\text{safe}} < 0.3  – system may be used as a decision‑support tool with human oversight.
· Supervised:  0.3 \le S_{\text{safe}} < 0.6  – system may operate with continuous human monitoring and intervention capability.
· Collaborative:  0.6 \le S_{\text{safe}} < 0.8  – system may operate with intermittent human oversight; human can take over if needed.
· Autonomous:  S_{\text{safe}} \ge 0.8  – system may operate without direct human supervision, subject to periodic audits and fail‑safe mechanisms.

These bands are derived from the interpretation thresholds used per dimension (Limited, Emerging, Proficient, Advanced) but applied to the minimum across tiers. This provides a single binding constraint for risk assessment.

