Appendix B: Properties of the Power Mean

B.1 Monotonicity and Continuity

Theorem B.1 (Monotonicity of Power Mean). For fixed \mathbf{D} \in (0,1]^n and weights \mathbf{w} with w_i > 0 and \sum_i w_i = 1, the function p \mapsto M_p(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) is continuous on \mathbb{R} \cup \{\pm\infty\} and non-decreasing.

Proof.

We proceed in three parts.

Part 1: Continuity at p = 0. For p \neq 0, write D_i^p = e^{p \ln D_i} = 1 + p \ln D_i + O(p^2) as p \to 0. Then:

\sum_i w_i D_i^p = 1 + p \sum_i w_i \ln D_i + O(p^2).

Taking logarithms and using \ln(1+x) = x + O(x^2):

\ln\left( \sum_i w_i D_i^p \right) = p \sum_i w_i \ln D_i + O(p^2).

Therefore:

\lim_{p \to 0} \frac{1}{p} \ln\left( \sum_i w_i D_i^p \right) = \sum_i w_i \ln D_i.

Exponentiating both sides:

\lim_{p \to 0} M_p(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) = \exp\left( \sum_i w_i \ln D_i \right) = \prod_i D_i^{w_i} = M_0(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}).

Continuity at p = 0 is established. For p \neq 0, continuity is immediate from the continuity of the exponential and power functions.

Part 2: Monotonicity for 0 < p < q. Let \varphi(t) = t^{q/p}, which is convex on t > 0 since q/p > 1. By Jensen's inequality applied to the weighted average of D_i^p:

\varphi\left( \sum_i w_i D_i^p \right) \leq \sum_i w_i \varphi(D_i^p),

i.e.,

\left( \sum_i w_i D_i^p \right)^{q/p} \leq \sum_i w_i D_i^q.

Raising both sides to the power 1/q (which preserves the inequality since 1/q > 0):

\left( \sum_i w_i D_i^p \right)^{1/p} \leq \left( \sum_i w_i D_i^q \right)^{1/q},

which is M_p \leq M_q.

Part 3: Extension to p < 0 and limits. For p < 0, use the reflection identity:

M_{-p}(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) = \frac{1}{M_p(1/\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w})},

where 1/\mathbf{D} = (1/D_1, \ldots, 1/D_n). This follows directly from the definition:

M_{-p}(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) = \left( \sum_i w_i D_i^{-p} \right)^{-1/p} = \left( \sum_i w_i (1/D_i)^p \right)^{-1/p} = \frac{1}{\left( \sum_i w_i (1/D_i)^p \right)^{1/p}} = \frac{1}{M_p(1/\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w})}.

Applying the 0 < p < q monotonicity to 1/\mathbf{D} and inverting reverses the inequality direction, establishing monotonicity across all of \mathbb{R}, including sign changes through p = 0 (continuity already established).

For the limits, let D_{\max} = \max_i D_i and let w_{\max} be the total weight on maximizing indices. Since D_i \leq D_{\max} for all i:

w_{\max}^{1/p} D_{\max} \leq M_p \leq D_{\max}.

As p \to \infty, w_{\max}^{1/p} \to 1, so M_p \to D_{\max} by the squeeze theorem. The p \to -\infty limit follows from the reflection identity. This completes the proof. \blacksquare

B.2 Well-Posedness of the Capability Balance Index

Theorem B.2. For any tier with dimension scores \mathbf{D} \in (0,1]^k and weights \mathbf{w} (with \sum_i w_i = 1, w_i > 0), the Capability Balance Index defined as

\text{CBI} = 1 - \frac{M_0}{M_1}

satisfies \text{CBI} \in [0,1], with \text{CBI} = 0 if and only if all D_i in the tier are equal.

Proof.

By Theorem B.1 with p = 0 < 1 = q, we have M_0 \leq M_1. Since all D_i \in (0,1], it follows that M_0 = \prod_i D_i^{w_i} \geq 0 and M_1 = \sum_i w_i D_i > 0 (assuming not all D_i = 0, which is excluded by D_i \in (0,1]). Therefore:

0 \leq \frac{M_0}{M_1} \leq 1 \implies \text{CBI} = 1 - \frac{M_0}{M_1} \in [0,1].

For equality M_0 = M_1, Jensen's inequality in the proof of Theorem B.1 must be tight. By strict convexity of \varphi(t) = t^{q/p} for q \neq p, equality holds if and only if D_i^p is w-almost-everywhere constant, i.e., all D_i with w_i > 0 are equal. This establishes the "if and only if" condition. \blacksquare

Remark B.3 (Tier-Level CBI). For the three-tier framework, the CBI is computed separately for each tier using within-tier weights renormalized to sum to 1. The tier-level CBI values are reported as a vector \text{CBI} = (\text{CBI}_1, \text{CBI}_2, \text{CBI}_3), not aggregated across tiers.

