Appendix D: Sensitivity Analysis

D.1 Parameter Sensitivity \Delta S_p

Proposition D.1. For a chosen p_{\text{chosen}} and a plausible interval [p_{\min}, p_{\max}] with p_{\min} \leq p_{\text{chosen}} \leq p_{\max}, the maximum deviation of the composite score under variation of p is:

\Delta S_p = \max\left( S(p_{\text{chosen}}) - S(p_{\min}),\; S(p_{\max}) - S(p_{\text{chosen}}) \right).

Proof.

By Theorem B.1, S(p) = M_p(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) is non-decreasing in p. Therefore:

· On [p_{\min}, p_{\text{chosen}}], the function g(p) = S(p_{\text{chosen}}) - S(p) is non-negative and non-increasing, so its maximum is at p = p_{\min}.
· On [p_{\text{chosen}}, p_{\max}], the function h(p) = S(p) - S(p_{\text{chosen}}) is non-negative and non-decreasing, so its maximum is at p = p_{\max}.

The overall maximum of |S(p) - S(p_{\text{chosen}})| over [p_{\min}, p_{\max}] is the larger of these two boundary values. \blacksquare

Practical implication. Only two additional function evaluations are needed: S(p_{\min}) and S(p_{\max}).

D.2 Weight Sensitivity \Delta S_w

Lemma D.2 (Convexity in Weights). For fixed \mathbf{D} \in (0,1]^n, the function \mathbf{w} \mapsto M_p(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) is convex in \mathbf{w} for every p \leq 1, and linear for p = 1.

Proof.

For p = 1, M_1(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) = \sum_i w_i D_i is linear in \mathbf{w}.

For p \neq 0, 1: Write L(\mathbf{w}) = \sum_i w_i D_i^p, which is linear in \mathbf{w}. Then M_p = L(\mathbf{w})^{1/p}. The scalar function x \mapsto x^{1/p} has second derivative \frac{1}{p}\left(\frac{1}{p} - 1\right)x^{1/p - 2}. For p < 0: 1/p < 0, so \frac{1}{p}\left(\frac{1}{p} - 1\right) = (\text{neg}) \cdot (\text{neg}) > 0, hence x \mapsto x^{1/p} is convex. Since L is linear (hence affine, and both convex and concave), the composition of a convex function with an affine function is convex. Therefore M_p is convex in \mathbf{w}.

For p = 0: M_0(\mathbf{w}) = \exp(\sum_i w_i \ln D_i) = \exp(\text{linear in } \mathbf{w}). The exponential function is convex and increasing, and the composition of a convex increasing function with an affine function is convex. Therefore M_0 is convex in \mathbf{w}. \blacksquare

Proposition D.3 (Vertex Reduction for Weight Sensitivity). Let \mathcal{W} = \text{conv}\{\mathbf{w}^{(1)}, \ldots, \mathbf{w}^{(m)}\} be the convex hull of m candidate weight vectors. For fixed \mathbf{D} and p \leq 1:

\max_{\mathbf{w} \in \mathcal{W}} S(p, \mathbf{w}) = \max_{k=1,\ldots,m} S(p, \mathbf{w}^{(k)}).

Proof.

By Lemma D.2, S(p, \mathbf{w}) = M_p(\mathbf{D}, \mathbf{w}) is convex in \mathbf{w} for p \leq 1. Let \mathbf{w}^* = \sum_k \lambda_k \mathbf{w}^{(k)} with \lambda_k \geq 0 and \sum_k \lambda_k = 1 be any point in \mathcal{W}. By Jensen's inequality (or the definition of convexity):

S(p, \mathbf{w}^*) \leq \sum_k \lambda_k S(p, \mathbf{w}^{(k)}) \leq \max_k S(p, \mathbf{w}^{(k)}).

Since \mathbf{w}^* was arbitrary, the maximum over \mathcal{W} is attained at a vertex. \blacksquare

Definition D.4 (Weight Sensitivity Interval). For a chosen weight vector \mathbf{w}_{\text{chosen}} and a polytope \mathcal{W} = \text{conv}\{\mathbf{w}^{(1)}, \ldots, \mathbf{w}^{(m)}\}, the weight sensitivity is:

\Delta S_w = \max_{k=1,\ldots,m} \left| S(p, \mathbf{w}^{(k)}) - S(p, \mathbf{w}_{\text{chosen}}) \right|.

Remark D.5 (Failure for p > 1). For p > 1, M_p is concave in \mathbf{w} (by a mirror-image second-derivative argument: \frac{1}{p}\left(\frac{1}{p} - 1\right) < 0 for p > 1), so the maximum can occur in the interior of \mathcal{W}. Since no current tier uses p > 1, this is not a live problem, but it should be flagged as a constraint on future tier redesigns.

Remark D.6 (Degeneracy for p \to -\infty). For the minimum aggregator, M_{-\infty} depends only on the support of positive weights, not their magnitudes. Weight sensitivity analysis is uninformative for this case. Therefore Tier 3 should use the harmonic mean (p = -1) when weight sensitivity is being reported.

D.3 Interpretation-Band Transition Probability

Definition D.7 (Interpretation Bands). The interpretation bands are defined by thresholds 0 < b_1 < b_2 < b_3 < 1 corresponding to "Limited," "Emerging," "Proficient," and "Advanced" capabilities. For concreteness, we use b_1 = 0.3, b_2 = 0.6, b_3 = 0.8 as described in the main text.

Estimator D.8 (Normal Approximation). Given a tier score estimate \hat{S} with standard error \widehat{\text{SE}}(S) = \sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}(S)}, the probability of the true score S falling below a boundary b is approximated by:

\mathbb{P}(S < b) \approx \Phi\left( \frac{b - \hat{S}}{\widehat{\text{SE}}(S)} \right).

The probability of crossing a boundary (i.e., being on the opposite side from the point estimate) is:

\mathbb{P}(\text{cross } b) = \begin{cases}
\Phi\left( \frac{b - \hat{S}}{\widehat{\text{SE}}(S)} \right) & \text{if } \hat{S} > b, \\
1 - \Phi\left( \frac{b - \hat{S}}{\widehat{\text{SE}}(S)} \right) & \text{if } \hat{S} \leq b.
\end{cases}

Estimator D.9 (Bootstrap). Resample tasks using a paired design that matches the covariance structure (i.e., resample the same task indices across dimensions, preserving correlations). For each bootstrap replicate b = 1, \ldots, B, compute the tier score S^{(b)}. The empirical probability of crossing a boundary b is:

\mathbb{P}(\text{cross } b) \approx \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B \mathbb{I}\left[ (S^{(b)} - b)(\hat{S} - b) < 0 \right],

where \mathbb{I}[\cdot] is the indicator function. This approach is preferred when any dimension score is near 0 or 1 under p \leq 0 aggregation, where the normal approximation is unreliable.

