Appendix H: Contamination Risk Tiering Protocol

H.1 Definition of Risk Levels

Definition H.1 (Contamination Risk Levels). Each task item is assigned one of three risk levels based on the outcome of contamination detection:

· Green: No detectable leakage via n-gram overlap, embedding similarity, and perplexity divergence. Item is included at full weight.
· Yellow: Marginal signals (e.g., perplexity slightly below baseline). Item is retained but flagged, and its contribution to the dimension score is down-weighted.
· Red: Confirmed leakage. Item is excluded from scoring.

H.2 Detection Methods

Method H.2 (n-Gram Overlap). For text-based tasks, compute the longest common substring or n-gram overlap between the task prompt/expected answer and any document in a curated corpus of known training data. If the overlap exceeds a threshold (e.g., 20 consecutive tokens or 80% n-gram overlap), flag as potential leakage.

Method H.3 (Embedding Similarity). For all task types, compute the cosine similarity between the task item's embedding and the nearest neighbors in a reference corpus. If the similarity exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.9), flag as potential leakage.

Method H.4 (Perplexity Divergence). For text-based tasks, compute the perplexity of the task item under the evaluated model and compare to the perplexity of held-out, non-contaminated text from the same domain. If the task item has significantly lower perplexity (e.g., more than 2 standard deviations below the mean), flag as potential leakage.

H.3 Weighting Scheme for Yellow Items

Definition H.5 (Yellow Down-Weighting). For a dimension D with n tasks, suppose m tasks are classified as Yellow. The dimension score is computed as:

D_{\text{final}} = \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{Green}} s_i + \gamma \sum_{i \in \text{Yellow}} s_i}{\sum_{i \in \text{Green}} 1 + \gamma \sum_{i \in \text{Yellow}} 1},

where s_i is the task-level score and \gamma \in (0,1) is the down-weighting factor (e.g., \gamma = 0.5). Green tasks are weighted at \gamma = 1.

Justification H.6. Yellow items carry uncertainty about whether they were contaminated. Down-weighting them reduces their influence on the dimension score while still using the information they provide. The choice of \gamma should be calibrated based on the false-negative rate of the detection methods; if detection is very sensitive, \gamma can be closer to 1.

H.4 Task Rotation and the 90% Deprecation Rule

Definition H.7 (Deprecation Rule). Any benchmark suite whose mean solve rate among top frontier models exceeds 90% is deprecated from the basket in the next evaluation cycle. It is replaced with newly constructed, out-of-distribution tasks that are validated for construct relevance and difficulty calibration.

Rationale H.8. The 90% threshold is a practical trigger that signals "the benchmark is saturated and no longer provides information." At this level of performance, the benchmark cannot distinguish between systems that are "very good" and "excellent," and it is highly likely to be contaminated or overfitted. Deprecating saturated benchmarks forces the evaluation community to continuously develop new, harder tasks, which is itself a form of adversarial robustness.

