Appendix M: Risk-Theoretic Justification for Aggregation (Detailed)

M.1 Loss Functions and Aggregation

Definition M.1 (Dimension Deficit). For a dimension i with score D_i \in [0,1], the deficit is \delta_i = 1 - D_i.

Definition M.2 (Loss Function). A loss function L: [0,1]^n \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} maps a vector of dimension deficits \boldsymbol{\delta} = (\delta_1, \ldots, \delta_n) to a non-negative real loss.

Definition M.3 (Separable Loss). A loss function is separable if it can be written as:

L(\boldsymbol{\delta}) = \sum_i w_i \ell(\delta_i),

for some univariate loss function \ell: [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} and weights w_i > 0 with \sum_i w_i = 1.

Proposition M.4 (Loss Convexity and Aggregation). Consider a separable loss function L(\boldsymbol{\delta}) = \sum_i w_i \ell(\delta_i). Then:

1. If \ell is convex, minimizing L subject to a fixed mean deficit \frac{1}{n}\sum_i \delta_i = c leads to concentrating the deficit in a single dimension. The appropriate aggregation is the minimum (weakest-link).
2. If \ell is linear, minimizing L subject to a fixed mean deficit is indifferent to the distribution of deficits across dimensions. The appropriate aggregation is the arithmetic mean.
3. If \ell is concave (sublinear), minimizing L subject to a fixed mean deficit leads to spreading the deficit evenly across all dimensions. The appropriate aggregation is the geometric mean.

Proof.

This is a standard result from the theory of convex risk measures. For convex \ell, the function \boldsymbol{\delta} \mapsto \sum_i w_i \ell(\delta_i) is convex, and by Jensen's inequality:

\ell\left( \sum_i w_i \delta_i \right) \leq \sum_i w_i \ell(\delta_i).

To minimize the right-hand side subject to a fixed mean, one concentrates the deficit where \ell is smallest (i.e., in one dimension), driving the others to zero deficit. The corresponding aggregation is the maximum deficit, which is equivalent to the minimum score. For concave \ell, the inequality reverses, and spreading the deficit evenly minimizes the loss. For linear \ell, equality holds, and any distribution of deficits yields the same loss. \blacksquare

Application to Tiers:

· Tier 3 (Robustness): The loss from a safety failure is convex (catastrophic consequences for even a single failure). Therefore p = -1 or -\infty (minimum).
· Tier 2 (Metacognition): The loss from miscalibration is approximately linear (inefficiency proportional to miscalibration). Therefore p = 1 (arithmetic mean).
· Tier 1 (Competence): The loss from imbalance is sublinear (diminishing returns to balance). Therefore p = 0 (geometric mean).

This risk-theoretic justification connects the framework to robust decision theory and makes the normative content of the aggregation choice explicit.

