Appendix N: Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)

N.1 Why not just use the arithmetic mean for everything?

The arithmetic mean allows high scores in some dimensions to fully compensate for low scores in others. In safety-critical contexts, this is problematic: a system that is excellent at causal reasoning but fails catastrophically in robustness should not receive a high overall score. The tiered aggregation with different p values ensures that compensability is calibrated to the risk profile of each tier.

N.2 How are the tier thresholds (0.3, 0.6, 0.8) chosen?

These thresholds are based on the heuristic that a system scoring below 0.3 has not demonstrated reliable competence, a system between 0.3 and 0.6 has demonstrated competence but not at human level, a system between 0.6 and 0.8 has demonstrated human-level or near-human-level competence, and a system above 0.8 has demonstrated superhuman or clearly proficient capability. These thresholds are subject to revision based on empirical calibration and stakeholder input. The key point is that they are applied to the Safety-Envelope Composite (minimum across tiers), not to any single tier.

N.3 What if a dimension cannot be measured for a particular system?

If a dimension cannot be measured (e.g., D₉ for a single system without a model family), that dimension is reported as "not available" and the tier score is computed over the available dimensions with renormalized weights. However, the Safety-Envelope Composite requires all three tier scores; if any tier is unavailable, the system cannot be certified for autonomous deployment (conservative default).

N.4 How does the framework handle non-linear interactions between dimensions?

The framework's dependence-adjustment procedure (Appendix F) addresses linear dependence through regression-based residualization. Non-linear interactions are not modeled in the current framework and are flagged as a direction for future work. The framework is explicit about this limitation.

N.5 What is the cost of full evaluation?

The full battery with private tasks, contamination auditing, sensitivity analysis, and conformal prediction is computationally expensive. A "core battery" consisting of Tier 1 dimensions plus a minimal safety screen (Tier 3, D₈) can be used for rapid screening, with full evaluation reserved for high-risk deployments. The exact cost depends on the number of tasks per dimension and the computational resources of the evaluation consortium.

