Appendix O: Glossary of Terms

Benefit-of-the-Doubt (BoD): A DEA-based composite indicator methodology where each evaluated unit selects its own weights to maximize its composite score subject to a common frontier constraint.

Capability Balance Index (CBI): A measure of disparity among dimension scores within a tier, defined as 1 - M_0/M_1.

Conformal Prediction: A distribution-free framework for constructing prediction intervals with finite-sample coverage guarantees.

Contamination: The phenomenon where a model's training data includes examples from a benchmark task, leading to inflated benchmark scores.

Cross-Efficiency: A DEA extension that scores each unit under the optimal weights of all other units, addressing the self-rating problem.

Data Envelopment Analysis (DEA): A non-parametric method for estimating the efficiency frontier of a set of decision-making units.

Delta Method: A statistical technique for approximating the variance of a function of a random variable using a first-order Taylor expansion.

Dimension: A distinct cognitive capability (e.g., causal reasoning, planning) measured by the evaluation battery.

Exchangeability: A property of random variables whose joint distribution is invariant under permutation.

Goodhart's Law: The principle that when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

Interpretation Bands: Threshold-based categories (Limited, Emerging, Proficient, Advanced) used to interpret numerical scores.

Power Mean: A family of averages parameterized by p that includes the arithmetic mean (p=1), geometric mean (p=0), harmonic mean (p=-1), minimum (p \to -\infty), and maximum (p \to \infty).

Reference Panel: A set of previously evaluated systems used for calibration, DEA frontier estimation, and dependence-adjustment regression.

Residualization: The process of removing the linear effect of one or more predictor variables from a response variable.

Safety-Envelope Composite: The minimum of the three tier scores, used as a deployment-relevant binding constraint.

Tier: A group of related dimensions aggregated with a common power-mean parameter.

